A||||4||fiero_hunter85||short shifter questions||||5||
Z||000000||fiero_hunter85||09-21-2001||02:10 PM||||i am thinking of going to the bone yard and getting another shifter to turn into a short shifter. before i make one i need to know 2 things. 1. are the 4 and 5 speed shifter posts the same? <BR>2. if the shifter is 2 inches shorter wont it be harder to shift?<P>i can easaly make a short shifter but my mother might be driving my car so i dont want it to be to hard to shift. <BR>i have a 4 speed if it matters.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero_hunter85 (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jncomutt||09-21-2001||02:26 PM||||I made a "short shifter" just by cutting out the shaft and rewelding it in.  Its much shorter yet has made no difference in shifting effort.<P>This is jus for appearence mainly.  While yes, it does shorten the distance the top of the shifter will move from gear to gear.  It still doesn't make it a True "Short throw" shifter as would changing the linkage and such.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||09-21-2001||06:31 PM||||I started making then with 5/8 SS rod to fit most after market shift knobs?<P><BR>DXR_DAD<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||09-21-2001||06:36 PM||||There are at least 2 different styles of shifter arms - one has 90 deg bends and the other has 2 45 deg bends, there is a web page around somewhere showing where to cut either type and reweld. I do almost the same thing as DXR_DAD but I just use a 5/8 bolt from the hard ware store.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-21-2001||07:05 PM||||You'll end up with the same throw if you only shorten the top.  My shifter from Rodney Dickman has the bottom either shortened or lengthened to shorten the throw.||||reg||1
